WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:05.580
He's played a young Mandalorian and a&nbsp;
Tusken Raider? This celebrity's nephew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.580 --> 00:00:08.880
is carving out a spot for himself&nbsp;
in Star Wars and so much more.

00:00:08.880 --> 00:00:13.620
As if there weren't enough reasons to check out&nbsp;
"The Book of Boba Fett," "Star Wars" fans needed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:18.180
to watch it to understand what's going on in&nbsp;
"The Mandalorian" Season 3. Over the course&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.180 --> 00:00:22.980
of a couple of episodes, we see Din Djarin&nbsp;
getting exiled from the Mandalorian way. He's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:27.900
also reunited with his little buddy Grogu, who's&nbsp;
been learning the ways of the Jedi. Basically,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:32.700
it sets up everything you need to know going&nbsp;
into a new season of "The Mandalorian." Needless&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.700 --> 00:00:36.480
to say, the first episode of Season 3&nbsp;
really kicks things off with a bang.

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:41.940
The episode opens with a traditional Mandalorian&nbsp;
baptism ceremony. The Armorer crafts a helmet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.940 --> 00:00:47.160
for a young Mandalorian child, who is eagerly&nbsp;
awaiting this rite of passage. Unfortunately,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.160 --> 00:00:53.580
the ceremony is interrupted by a giant monster,&nbsp;
as often happens in a galaxy far, far away. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:57.840
the monster may not have been the only thing to&nbsp;
catch your attention. The child getting baptized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:02.520
may have looked familiar to you, especially&nbsp;
if you tune into a lot of Disney+ originals.

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:07.680
The young actor's name is Wesley Kimmel,&nbsp;
and that last name is no coincidence. He is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.680 --> 00:01:10.860
indeed, the nephew of late-night&nbsp;
talk show host Jimmy Kimmel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:13.560
but he's quickly becoming&nbsp;
an actor in his own right.

00:01:13.560 --> 00:01:18.000
Wesley's resume is building up nicely.&nbsp;
While he initially appeared periodically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:22.740
on episodes of "Jimmy Kimmel Live!," he's&nbsp;
since popped up in various films and TV shows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.740 --> 00:01:27.300
including "The Rookie" and "Good Girls."&nbsp;
However, he appears to be carving out a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.300 --> 00:01:32.160
nice niche for himself on Disney+. He stars&nbsp;
as the Commercial Boy in a few episodes of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:36.180
"WandaVision," and he played a Tusken Kid&nbsp;
on the first three episodes of "The Book of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.180 --> 00:01:40.680
Boba Fett." He must've made a good impression&nbsp;
on somebody, as now he's rejoined "Star Wars"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.680 --> 00:01:44.940
for "Chapter 17" of "The Mandalorian."
 
Most recently, Wesley Kimmel could be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:49.320
found playing Jack in the Netflix original&nbsp;
film "Your Place or Mine," starring Reese&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:53.400
Witherspoon and Ashton Kutcher. It's also&nbsp;
been confirmed that he'll appear in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:58.200
upcoming Amazon Prime Video movie "Red One,"&nbsp;
so he's certainly keeping busy these days.

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:02.400
"It was dope. My mom would've&nbsp;
never let me watch that."

00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:07.020
Wesley's inclusion in two "Star Wars" properties&nbsp;
is made possible by the fact that viewers didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.020 --> 00:02:11.640
get a chance to see his face in "The Book&nbsp;
of Boba Fett." He's a child Tusken Raider,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:16.560
and as such, he wears a mask obstructing his face&nbsp;
for any scenes he's featured in. This makes it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:21.660
easy for the young actor to come back to play a&nbsp;
Mandalorian child in "The Mandalorian" Season 3.

00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:25.440
In many ways, "The Book of Boba Fett"&nbsp;
turned into "The Mandalorian" Season 2.5,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:30.960
thanks to the inclusion of fan favorites Mando&nbsp;
and Grogu. But one thing it did exceptionally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:35.760
well was flesh out the Tusken Raiders as&nbsp;
actual characters as opposed to plot fodder,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:38.640
and they played an integral&nbsp;
role in Boba Fett's journey.

00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:43.440
Rory Ross, who plays one of the Tusken Raiders&nbsp;
on the show, opened up about what it was like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:47.700
to be part of such a massive "Star Wars"&nbsp;
property. In an interview with Looper,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:47.700 --> 00:02:51.360
Ross actually gave a shout out to the&nbsp;
young Kimmel for playing the Tusken kid.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:55.680
Pointing out how important the character&nbsp;
was for Boba Fett's arc, Ross told Looper,

00:02:55.680 --> 00:03:01.260
"The little Tusken kid, played by Wesley Kimmel,&nbsp;
represents Boba as a child and him, his innocence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:06.180
and everything else when his father passes&nbsp;
away and we see that on Geonosis in Episode 2."

00:03:06.180 --> 00:03:09.180
It remains to be seen if the&nbsp;
Mandalorian child will factor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:12.660
into the larger machinations of&nbsp;
the show during Season 3. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.660 --> 00:03:16.680
it's already clear that Wesley Kimmel is&nbsp;
proving to be a valuable asset to Disney.

